4. EXACT MONEY AND DATA MODEL RULES
==================================================
These rules take priority over visual design:
A. Store all BDT amounts as signed Kotlin Long units of POISHA (100 poisha = ৳1). Never use Float/Double for monetary storage or totals. Parse inputs safely using integer/decimal-safe parsing (e.g. BigDecimal with exact scale checks) and convert to Long with overflow protection. Display amounts with currency-aware formatting; changing language must NEVER change their numeric value.
B. Version 1 supports BDT only. Keep a currency field of "BDT" where needed, but do not offer unsupported multi-currency conversions.
C. Every record uses immutable UUID IDs, UTC timestamps, proper updatedAt and optional deletedAt tombstones where needed. Convert to Asia/Dhaka for month/day reports unless the user changes the display timezone. Local month boundary calculations must handle daylight and date boundaries safely.
D. Opening balances are NOT income. Income adds money, expense subtracts money, transfer subtracts from one account and adds to another, balance adjustment changes the account balance with an audited reason and explicit confirmation. A transfer is NEVER counted as new income or spending.
E. Model transfers as one uniquely identified operation with source account, destination account, amount, timestamp and optional notes. Use one Room @Transaction atomic operation; reject source=destination, nonpositive amount and invalid account references. All affected screens update reactively.
F. Current account balance is derived from opening balance plus valid income, minus valid expenses, plus incoming transfers, minus outgoing transfers, plus signed adjustments. Do not independently mutate a saved "currentBalance" field in ways that drift from the ledger. Total Balance is the sum of active + included accounts according to an explicit policy. Archived accounts remain in history and are not silently discarded.
G. "Net Cash Flow" for a period = income - expenses; do not confuse it with savings-goal allocations or opening balances. If the UI says "Net Savings," explain that it means period income minus expenses, not money physically transferred into a savings account.
H. Never silently delete finance history. Transaction delete uses confirmation and a soft-delete/tombstone strategy compatible with backup merging. Edits to transfers and adjustments must be atomic and leave a consistent ledger.
I. Prevent orphaned IDs and broken foreign keys. Archive categories/accounts with history; do not cascade-delete users' transactions. Define migrations from the beginning; never use destructive migration in production.
J. Use deterministic rounding, stable sort/filter results, safe SQL aggregation and efficient indexing for thousands of records.

Suggested Room tables (adapt if a safer normalized schema is justified): AccountEntity, CategoryEntity, TransactionEntity, TransferEntity, BalanceAdjustmentEntity, BudgetEntity, SavingsGoalEntity, SavingsAllocationEntity, RecurringTemplateEntity, RecurringOccurrenceEntity, BackupMetadataEntity, AlertHistoryEntity, plus relevant preference models in DataStore. Add indices and foreign keys; schema versions and migrations must be included.

