12. LOCAL PRIVACY, LOCK AND CRYPTOGRAPHY
==================================================
- Room database resides in the app's private Android sandbox. Do NOT falsely describe it as encrypted-at-rest merely because it is private or protected by App Lock. If encrypted database storage is not explicitly implemented and tested, disclose this correctly.
- Optional App Lock uses BiometricPrompt or supported device credentials with proper cancellation/recovery behavior. It must not block data backup or crash on unsupported phones.
- Protect credentials/tokens and optional locally remembered encryption material with appropriate Android Keystore facilities. Never embed secrets, passphrases or refresh tokens in source code; never log financial data.
- Backups use a documented, versioned binary envelope: format version, KDF identifier/parameters, random unique salt, fresh random 96-bit GCM nonce, authenticated metadata, encrypted payload and integrity information.
- Prefer a mature supported KDF (e.g. PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256 using carefully chosen, sufficiently strong device-compatible iterations; Argon2id only if a maintained compatible library is available). Use AES-256-GCM and CSPRNG. Encryption must be based on a user-controlled BACKUP PASSPHRASE, not exclusively on a non-exportable Keystore key, so new-device recovery is possible.
- Compress/serialize the validated snapshot BEFORE encryption if compression is enabled. Defend against oversized/corrupt inputs and resource exhaustion during import. Clean up plaintext temp files and zero sensitive memory where practical.
- A lost backup passphrase cannot be magically recovered: explain this before the first encrypted export.

